---
title: "Webhooks"
description: "Verify webhook deliveries and act on the message an event names with the Photon API clients."
---

Photon sends your project's events to an HTTPS endpoint you control, as signed
`POST` requests that follow [Standard Webhooks](https://www.standardwebhooks.com/).
Each client verifies a delivery with one call. After that, the same client
reads and answers the message the event names.

This page covers the client side. [Webhooks](/message-delivery/webhook) covers
delivery: retries, ordering, duplicate deliveries, and the dashboard.

## Create a destination

A destination is the URL Photon delivers to and the events it receives there.
Create one with a project API key. The response holds the destination's signing
secret, `whsec_...`. Photon returns it only in this response, in a repeat of
the request with the same `Idempotency-Key`, and when you rotate it. Store it
in your secret manager, for example as `PHOTON_WEBHOOK_SECRET`. It is not an
API key: it only verifies deliveries.

The Rust example uses the [`tokio`](https://crates.io/crates/tokio),
[`serde_json`](https://crates.io/crates/serde_json), and
[`rand`](https://crates.io/crates/rand) crates; add them with
`cargo add tokio --features macros,rt-multi-thread` and `cargo add serde_json rand`.

<CodeGroup>
```typescript TypeScript
import { randomUUID } from "node:crypto";
import { Photon } from "@photon-ai/api";

const apiKey = process.env.PHOTON_PROJECT_API_KEY;
const projectId = process.env.PHOTON_PROJECT_ID;
if (!apiKey || !projectId) {
  throw new Error("Set PHOTON_PROJECT_API_KEY and PHOTON_PROJECT_ID");
}

const photon = new Photon({
  headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${apiKey}` },
});

const created = await photon.projects.createWebhookDestination({
  path: { projectId },
  headers: { idempotencyKey: randomUUID() },
  body: {
    name: "Support inbox",
    url: "https://example.com/webhooks/photon",
    apiVersion: "2026-07-01",
    enabledEvents: ["message.received"],
  },
});
console.log(created.destination.destinationId);
// created.signingSecret is the destination's whsec_ secret. Store it in your
// secret manager now, for example as PHOTON_WEBHOOK_SECRET.
```

```python Python
import os
import uuid

from photon_api import Photon
from photon_api.rpc_generated import CreateWebhookDestinationInput

api_key = os.environ["PHOTON_PROJECT_API_KEY"]
project_id = os.environ["PHOTON_PROJECT_ID"]

with Photon(headers={"Authorization": f"Bearer {api_key}"}) as photon:
    created = photon.projects.create_webhook_destination(
        CreateWebhookDestinationInput.model_validate(
            {
                "path": {"projectId": project_id},
                "headers": {"Idempotency-Key": str(uuid.uuid4())},
                "body": {
                    "name": "Support inbox",
                    "url": "https://example.com/webhooks/photon",
                    "apiVersion": "2026-07-01",
                    "enabledEvents": ["message.received"],
                },
            }
        )
    )
    print(created.destination.destinationId)
    # created.signingSecret is the destination's whsec_ secret. Store it in your
    # secret manager now, for example as PHOTON_WEBHOOK_SECRET.
```

```rust Rust
use photon_ai_api::{Credential, PhotonClientBuilder, SecretString, types};

#[tokio::main]
async fn main() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {
    let api_key = std::env::var("PHOTON_PROJECT_API_KEY")?;
    let project_id = std::env::var("PHOTON_PROJECT_ID")?;

    let credential = Credential::Bearer(SecretString::from(api_key));
    let client = PhotonClientBuilder::new()
        .credential("projectApiKey", credential)
        .build()?;

    let body: types::CreateWebhookDestinationRequest = serde_json::from_value(serde_json::json!({
        "name": "Support inbox",
        "url": "https://example.com/webhooks/photon",
        "apiVersion": "2026-07-01",
        "enabledEvents": ["message.received"],
    }))?;
    let idempotency_key = format!("{:032x}", rand::random::<u128>());
    let created = client
        .create_webhook_destination(idempotency_key, project_id, &body)
        .await
        .map_err(|error| format!("{error:?}"))?
        .into_inner();
    println!("{}", created.destination.destination_id);
    // created.signing_secret is the destination's whsec_ secret. Store it in
    // your secret manager now, for example as PHOTON_WEBHOOK_SECRET.
    Ok(())
}
```

```go Go
package main

import (
	"context"
	"encoding/json"
	"fmt"
	"log"
	"os"

	photonapi "github.com/photon-hq/api/packages/go"
)

func main() {
	apiKey := os.Getenv("PHOTON_PROJECT_API_KEY")
	projectID := os.Getenv("PHOTON_PROJECT_ID")
	if apiKey == "" || projectID == "" {
		log.Fatal("Set PHOTON_PROJECT_API_KEY and PHOTON_PROJECT_ID")
	}

	client := photonapi.NewClient(photonapi.WithAPIKey(apiKey))

	var body photonapi.CreateWebhookDestinationRequest
	if err := json.Unmarshal([]byte(`{
		"name": "Support inbox",
		"url": "https://example.com/webhooks/photon",
		"apiVersion": "2026-07-01",
		"enabledEvents": ["message.received"]
	}`), &body); err != nil {
		log.Fatal(err)
	}
	// IdempotencyKey is left empty, so the client generates one.
	created, err := client.Projects.CreateWebhookDestination(context.Background(), &photonapi.CreateWebhookDestinationParams{
		ProjectID: projectID,
		Body:      body,
	})
	if err != nil {
		log.Fatal(err)
	}
	fmt.Println(created.Destination.DestinationID)
	// created.SigningSecret is the destination's whsec_ secret. Store it in your
	// secret manager now, for example as PHOTON_WEBHOOK_SECRET.
}
```
</CodeGroup>

## Receive a delivery

A receiver verifies the request before it does anything else. When verification
fails, it answers `400` and ignores the body. When it succeeds, the body is the
event: here, a `message.received` event, whose `data` names the project and
the message. The receiver reads the message with `getMessage`, marks it read
with `markMessageRead`, and answers `204`.

Each example is a handler you call from your web framework's route. The Go
example is an `http.Handler`. The Rust example takes an `http::HeaderMap`, as
axum and hyper provide, and uses the [`http`](https://crates.io/crates/http)
and [`serde_json`](https://crates.io/crates/serde_json) crates. A secret that
is not a `whsec_...` signing secret is the receiver's own misconfiguration, not
a bad delivery: the Rust example checks it once when the receiver starts, and
the others answer it with `500` rather than `400`.

<CodeGroup>
```typescript TypeScript
import { Photon, verifyWebhook, WebhookVerificationError } from "@photon-ai/api";

const apiKey = process.env.PHOTON_PROJECT_API_KEY;
const secret = process.env.PHOTON_WEBHOOK_SECRET;
if (!apiKey || !secret) {
  throw new Error("Set PHOTON_PROJECT_API_KEY and PHOTON_WEBHOOK_SECRET");
}

const photon = new Photon({
  headers: { Authorization: `Bearer ${apiKey}` },
});

// Call this from your HTTP route with the incoming request, and send the
// response it returns.
export const handleDelivery = async (request: Request): Promise<Response> => {
  // Verify the body exactly as it arrived, before anything parses it.
  const body = await request.text();
  try {
    verifyWebhook(body, request.headers, { secret });
  } catch (error) {
    if (error instanceof WebhookVerificationError) {
      return new Response(null, { status: 400 });
    }
    throw error;
  }

  // The fields this receiver reads: every event's id and type, and the message
  // and project a message.received event names.
  const event = JSON.parse(body) as {
    id: string;
    type: string;
    data: { projectId: string; object: { id: string } };
  };
  if (event.type === "message.received") {
    const path = { id: event.data.projectId, messageId: event.data.object.id };
    const message = await photon.projects.getMessage({ path });
    console.log(`Received a message on ${message?.platform}`);
    await photon.projects.markMessageRead({
      path,
      // One key per event, so a retried delivery repeats this request.
      headers: { "idempotency-key": `read-${event.id}` },
    });
  }
  return new Response(null, { status: 204 });
};
```

```python Python
import json
import os
from collections.abc import Mapping

from photon_api import Photon, WebhookVerificationError, verify_webhook
from photon_api.rpc_generated import GetMessageInput, MarkMessageReadInput

api_key = os.environ["PHOTON_PROJECT_API_KEY"]
secret = os.environ["PHOTON_WEBHOOK_SECRET"]

photon = Photon(headers={"Authorization": f"Bearer {api_key}"})


def handle_delivery(body: bytes, headers: Mapping[str, str]) -> int:
    """Call this from your HTTP route with the raw body and the headers, and
    answer with the status it returns."""
    # Verify the body exactly as it arrived, before anything parses it.
    try:
        verify_webhook(body, headers, secret=secret)
    except WebhookVerificationError:
        return 400

    event = json.loads(body)
    if event["type"] == "message.received":
        path = {"id": event["data"]["projectId"], "messageId": event["data"]["object"]["id"]}
        message = photon.projects.get_message(GetMessageInput.model_validate({"path": path}))
        if message is not None:
            print("Received a message on", message.model_dump(mode="json")["platform"])
        photon.projects.mark_message_read(
            MarkMessageReadInput.model_validate(
                # One key per event, so a retried delivery repeats this request.
                {"path": path, "headers": {"idempotency-key": f"read-{event['id']}"}}
            )
        )
    return 204
```

```rust Rust
use std::error::Error;

use http::HeaderMap;
use photon_ai_api::{
    Client, Credential, GetMessageResponse, PhotonClientBuilder, SecretString, WebhookSecret,
    verify_webhook,
};

struct Receiver {
    client: Client,
    secret: WebhookSecret,
}

impl Receiver {
    fn from_env() -> Result<Self, Box<dyn Error>> {
        let api_key = std::env::var("PHOTON_PROJECT_API_KEY")?;
        // A secret that is not a whsec_ signing secret is this receiver's
        // misconfiguration: it stops here, before any delivery arrives.
        let secret = WebhookSecret::new(&std::env::var("PHOTON_WEBHOOK_SECRET")?)?;
        let credential = Credential::Bearer(SecretString::from(api_key));
        let client = PhotonClientBuilder::new()
            .credential("projectApiKey", credential)
            .build()?;
        Ok(Self { client, secret })
    }

    /// Call this from your HTTP route with the raw body and the headers, and
    /// answer with the status it returns.
    async fn handle(&self, body: &[u8], headers: &HeaderMap) -> u16 {
        // Verify the body exactly as it arrived, before anything parses it.
        if verify_webhook(body, headers, &self.secret).is_err() {
            return 400;
        }
        match self.process(body).await {
            Ok(()) => 204,
            Err(error) => {
                eprintln!("{error}");
                500
            }
        }
    }

    async fn process(&self, body: &[u8]) -> Result<(), Box<dyn Error>> {
        let event: serde_json::Value = serde_json::from_slice(body)?;
        if event["type"] != "message.received" {
            return Ok(());
        }
        let event_id = event["id"].as_str().ok_or("no event id")?;
        let project_id = event["data"]["projectId"].as_str().ok_or("no projectId")?;
        let message_id = event["data"]["object"]["id"]
            .as_str()
            .ok_or("no message id")?;
        let response = self
            .client
            .get_message(project_id.to_owned(), message_id.to_owned(), None)
            .await
            .map_err(|error| format!("{error:?}"))?
            .into_inner();
        if let GetMessageResponse::Status200(message) = response {
            let message = serde_json::to_value(&message)?;
            println!("Received a message on {}", message["platform"]);
        }
        // One key per event, so a retried delivery repeats this request.
        self.client
            .mark_message_read(
                project_id.to_owned(),
                message_id.to_owned(),
                format!("read-{event_id}"),
            )
            .await
            .map_err(|error| format!("{error:?}"))?;
        Ok(())
    }
}
```

```go Go
package main

import (
	"encoding/json"
	"errors"
	"fmt"
	"io"
	"log"
	"net/http"
	"os"

	photonapi "github.com/photon-hq/api/packages/go"
)

func main() {
	receiver, err := newReceiver()
	if err != nil {
		log.Fatal(err)
	}
	http.Handle("POST /webhooks/photon", receiver)
	log.Fatal(http.ListenAndServe(":8080", nil))
}

func newReceiver() (http.Handler, error) {
	apiKey := os.Getenv("PHOTON_PROJECT_API_KEY")
	secret := os.Getenv("PHOTON_WEBHOOK_SECRET")
	if apiKey == "" || secret == "" {
		return nil, errors.New("set PHOTON_PROJECT_API_KEY and PHOTON_WEBHOOK_SECRET")
	}
	client := photonapi.NewClient(photonapi.WithAPIKey(apiKey))

	return http.HandlerFunc(func(w http.ResponseWriter, r *http.Request) {
		// Verify the body exactly as it arrived, before anything parses it.
		body, err := io.ReadAll(r.Body)
		if err != nil {
			http.Error(w, "unreadable body", http.StatusBadRequest)
			return
		}
		err = photonapi.VerifyWebhook(body, r.Header, secret)
		var invalid *photonapi.WebhookVerificationError
		if errors.As(err, &invalid) {
			http.Error(w, "invalid signature", http.StatusBadRequest)
			return
		}
		if err != nil {
			// ErrInvalidWebhookSecret: this receiver's misconfiguration, not a bad delivery.
			http.Error(w, "webhook secret misconfigured", http.StatusInternalServerError)
			return
		}

		// The fields this receiver reads: every event's id and type, and the
		// message and project a message.received event names.
		var event struct {
			ID   string `json:"id"`
			Type string `json:"type"`
			Data struct {
				ProjectID string `json:"projectId"`
				Object    struct {
					ID string `json:"id"`
				} `json:"object"`
			} `json:"data"`
		}
		if err := json.Unmarshal(body, &event); err != nil {
			http.Error(w, "invalid event", http.StatusBadRequest)
			return
		}
		if event.Type == "message.received" {
			message, err := client.Projects.GetMessage(r.Context(), &photonapi.GetMessageParams{
				ID:        event.Data.ProjectID,
				MessageID: event.Data.Object.ID,
			})
			if err != nil {
				http.Error(w, err.Error(), http.StatusInternalServerError)
				return
			}
			fmt.Println("Received a message on", message.Platform())
			_, err = client.Projects.MarkMessageRead(r.Context(), &photonapi.MarkMessageReadParams{
				ID:        event.Data.ProjectID,
				MessageID: event.Data.Object.ID,
				// One key per event, so a retried delivery repeats this request.
				IdempotencyKey: "read-" + event.ID,
			})
			if err != nil {
				http.Error(w, err.Error(), http.StatusInternalServerError)
				return
			}
		}
		w.WriteHeader(http.StatusNoContent)
	}), nil
}
```
</CodeGroup>

A few things the examples rely on:

- **The raw body.** The signature covers the body byte for byte. Read it before
  any JSON middleware parses it, and pass it unchanged. A body parsed and
  serialized again does not verify.
- **The event ID.** The event's `id` is the same on every retry of the event.
  The examples derive the `markMessageRead` idempotency key from it, so a
  retried delivery repeats the same request. Use it the same way to skip events
  you already handled.
- **The message's current state.** The event's `data.object` is the message as
  it was when Photon sent the event. `getMessage` reads it as it is now, as the
  client's typed `PlatformMessage`.

## Verify a delivery

| Language | Call | When the delivery does not verify |
| --- | --- | --- |
| TypeScript | `verifyWebhook(body, headers, { secret })`: `body` a `string` or `Uint8Array`, `headers` a `Headers` or a record such as Node's `IncomingHttpHeaders` | Throws `WebhookVerificationError`, a `PhotonError` |
| Python | `verify_webhook(body, headers, secret=secret)`: `body` `bytes` or `str`, `headers` a `Mapping[str, str]` | Raises `WebhookVerificationError`, a `PhotonError` |
| Rust | `verify_webhook(body, &headers, &secret)`: `body` a `&[u8]`, `headers` an `http::HeaderMap`, `secret` a `WebhookSecret` | Returns `Err(WebhookVerificationError)`: `InvalidSignature` or `TimestampOutOfRange` |
| Go | `VerifyWebhook(body, r.Header, secret)`: `body` a `[]byte`, `r.Header` an `http.Header` | Returns a `*WebhookVerificationError` |

A call that returns without an error has verified the delivery, and the body is
the event. A secret that is not a `whsec_...` signing secret is a configuration
error, not a bad delivery, and each client reports it apart: a `TypeError` in
TypeScript, a `ValueError` in Python, `InvalidWebhookSecret` from
`WebhookSecret::new` in Rust, and `ErrInvalidWebhookSecret` in Go.

Each call takes one secret, the destination's own. A receiver for several
destinations gives each one its own route and verifies with that destination's
secret.

### The signature

Each delivery carries three headers:

| Header | Value |
| --- | --- |
| `webhook-id` | The event's `id`. It does not change on retries. |
| `webhook-timestamp` | When Photon sent this attempt, in Unix seconds. Photon updates it on each retry. |
| `webhook-signature` | One or more space-separated `v1,<base64>` signatures. |

A signature is the Base64 HMAC-SHA256 of `<webhook-id>.<webhook-timestamp>.<body>`,
keyed with the Base64-decoded part of the secret after `whsec_`. A delivery
verifies when one of its signatures matches and its timestamp is within five
minutes of your server's clock, in the past or the future. Keep the clock
synchronized.

The clients verify with the official Standard Webhooks libraries:
`standardwebhooks` on npm, PyPI, and crates.io, and
`github.com/standard-webhooks/standard-webhooks/libraries` in Go. Any other
Standard Webhooks library verifies the same deliveries.

### Rotate the secret

Rotating a destination's secret returns a new one. For an overlap period, Photon
signs each delivery with both the new and the previous secret, so a receiver
that knows either one verifies it. The rotation request's `overlapSeconds` sets
that period, from 0 to 604800 seconds (seven days), and it is 86400 (one day)
by default; the response's `previousSecretExpiresAt` says when it ends. Switch
the receiver to the new secret before the previous one expires.
